<p>All basmati for export must also clear the legal floor in APEDA's contract-registration trade notice: grain longer than 6.61mm and a length-to-breadth ratio above 3.5. Both 1121 and Pusa clear it easily, so the floor matters mainly as a check on lots sold as basmati that are not.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The document most specific to basmati is the APEDA Registration-cum-Allocation Certificate (RCAC). Under DGFT Notification No. 18/2015-20, basmati export is allowed only once the contract is registered with APEDA. The exporter uploads the LC, contract or proforma invoice, and the RCAC it receives is valid for shipment for 45 days. So your LC or contract should be in place before we plan the loading date. The same trade notice sets destination-specific conditions for some markets. These include pre-shipment inspection for Iran, a certificate of inspection from the Export Inspection Council or an Export Inspection Agency for EU member states and the UK, Iceland, Liechtenstein, Norway and Switzerland, and conditions for the USA, China, Indonesia and Russia. Tell us your destination and we will map the right documents to the order.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>How the grade choices stack up in the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Basmati pricing is layered, and each specification choice adds or removes a layer. Variety is the largest: 1121 sits above 1509 and 1401 because of its grain length and elongation. Processing adds cost with each step. Sella and golden sella involve parboiling, drying and extra handling, so they usually price differently from raw rice of the same variety. Aging adds a holding cost and ties up stock for 12 or 24 months, so 24-month material costs more than 12-month, and 12-month more than fresh crop. Within a single variety and process, the broken-percentage level is the biggest step.</p>
<p>Outside the specification, prices follow the paddy market. Basmati is harvested October-December across the GI area of Punjab, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Delhi, western Uttar Pradesh and parts of Jammu &amp; Kashmir. The size and quality of each crop, the prices mills pay for paddy in the new-crop season, rupee exchange rates and freight to your port all move the number. Changes in Indian export policy can also move it quickly. Demand from the big Gulf and Iranian buyers, which take the largest shares, sets the tone for the whole market.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your variety, processing type, aging, broken percentage, packaging and destination port. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us all six.</p>
