<h2>What goes wrong on basmati grade orders, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p><strong>Grain length measured on the wrong basis.</strong> A certificate stating "8.35mm" means little unless you know how it was measured. The useful figure is the average length of a stated number of whole grains, before cooking, with broken grains excluded. Buyers who get a few hand-picked grains measured at inspection and later find a shorter average in the bags have not been cheated on the variety. The contract just never defined the method. Write the basis into the purchase order.</p>
<p><strong>Blending of varieties.</strong> 1121 commands a premium, so the commercial temptation is to blend in shorter Pusa or non-basmati long grain. Purity (92-95% of the named variety is typical) is the clause that protects you. A pre-shipment sample counted against it, by your nominated inspector if you prefer, is the practical check. The mix shows up as a wide spread of grain lengths within one sample.</p>
<p><strong>"Aged" without a number.</strong> Offers describing rice as "aged" or "old crop" without months have no enforceable meaning. Ask for 12 or 24 months in the contract, and ask how the stock was held. Rice kept in hot, damp conditions does not improve the way properly stored rice does.</p>
<p><strong>Sella colour mismatch.</strong> Sella and golden sella vary in shade between mills and batches. A retail buyer whose packs sit next to an earlier shipment will notice the difference. If colour matters for your shelf, approve a reference sample and hold later shipments to it.</p>
<p><strong>RCAC timing.</strong> Because the APEDA registration is valid for 45 days, an LC that arrives late or a contract that changes after registration can push a shipment past its window. Finalise quantity and terms before loading is planned.</p>
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

